1B1/1p1p4/2pP1B2/2P2NP1/PPQ2PK1/3R3R b - - 0 22","from":"d3","to":"g6"},"finalFen":"7r/5kbQ/p3q2R/1p1pr1B1/2p5/2P3P1/PP3PK1/8 w - - 5 36"},"white":"nihalsarin","black":"Njal28","labels":["book","book","book","book","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","good","best","best","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","good","best","best","best","excellent","good","good","excellent","best","good","good","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","best","mistake","brilliant","best","blunder","great","good","great","best","best","good","good","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","best","mistake","inaccuracy","inaccuracy","best","best","best","best","best","excellent","best","best","great"],"moments":[{"ply":42,"name":"21…c4","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"21…c4 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 45% to 31%.","points":["White answers 22.Bxg6, which takes the pawn on g6.","Better was 21…cxd4 (+0.51), which takes the pawn on d4."],"better":"21…cxd4 stops dxc5. Engine line: 22.cxd4 Rc8 23.Qe2 Nc4 24.Bh6 Qe7 (+0.51)."},{"ply":43,"name":"22.Bxg6","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"22.Bxg6!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the bishop on g6 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 22…fxg6 23.Qxg6 Nf8 24.Qg4 the engine rates it +2.45.","Takes the pawn on g6.","22.Bxg6 trades off the attacked bishop instead of losing it to …cxd3.","Black was threatening …cxd3, winning a bishop. The bishop takes something with it instead.","Takes the g6 pawn."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"22.Bxg6!! offers the bishop on g6. Taking it still leaves White a little better."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"22.Bxg6 puts the bishop on g6 where it can be taken by the f7 pawn. That's a bishop on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the bishop","text":"22…fxg6 is met by 23.Qxg6, taking the g6 pawn and threatening Ng5, winning the queen for a knight. Then 24.Qg4 threatens Rh6, winning a pawn, and 25.Bg5 threatens Bxd8, winning the queen for a bishop. White is a pawn down: White is a little better (+1.49). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 22.Be2, is clearly weaker (+0.98 against +2.45). Black answers 22…Nf8. That would leave White a little better."}]},{"ply":45,"name":"23.Bg5","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"23.Bg5 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 71% to 39%.","points":["Black answers 23…Qc7, which moves the attacked queen to safety.","Better was 23.Qxg6 (+2.40), which takes the pawn on g6."],"better":"23.Qxg6 wins a pawn and threatens Ng5, winning the queen for a knight. After 23…Nf8 24.Qg4 Nbd7 25.Bg5 Nf6 26.Bxf6 Qxf6 27.Rh5, White comes out a pawn up (+2.40)."},{"ply":46,"name":"23…Qc7","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"23…Qc7! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moves the attacked queen to safety.","Connects the rooks: development is complete and they can now support each other.","The next best move, 23…Re7, would drop Black's winning chances from 61% to 34% (+1.86).","23…Qc7 gets the queen out of the way of Bxd8.","White was threatening Bxd8, winning the queen for a bishop. The queen steps away.","White answers 24.Bf4, winning two pawns."]},{"ply":48,"name":"24…Nf8","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"24…Nf8! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the queen.","Shores up the e6 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 24…Nc8, would drop Black's winning chances from 63% to 8% (+6.71).","24…Nf8 threatens …Nxg6, winning the queen, and stops Qh7+.","It threatens …Nxg6, winning the queen, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White was threatening Qh7+, with a dangerous check. Nf8 takes care of it."]},{"ply":59,"name":"30.Rf4","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"30.Rf4 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 21% to 10%.","points":["Black answers 30…Qb7: the queen lines up with the White king, behind the d5 pawn.","Better was 30.Bh6 (−3.65), which moves the attacked bishop to safety."],"better":"30.Bh6 gets the bishop out of the way of …Rxg5. After 30…Ng6 31.R4h2 Rae8 32.Bxg7 Qxg7 33.Qd2, the bishops are traded (−3.65)."},{"ply":70,"name":"35…Rh8","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"35…Rh8! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Skewers the queen: when it moves, the rook on h6 behind it falls.","The rook takes the open h-file, aiming at the queen on h7.","The next best move, 35…Qf5, would drop Black's winning chances from 92% to 64% (−1.54).","35…Rh8 skewers the queen and the rook on h6 and takes the open h-file, aiming at the queen on h7.","Attacks the queen.","White answers 36.Rxe6, taking the queen on e6."]}],"opening":{"name":"East Indian Defense","eco":"A48","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 2…Nf6: 4 moves in all, 2 by White and 2 by Black.","White left the book with 3.e4. In 26,386 games from this position, strong players played 3.c4 (35%), 3.Bf4 (16%), 3.g3 (14%) or 3.Bg5 (12%).","The main line goes on 3.c4 Bg7 4.Nc3 O-O 5.e4 d6 6.Be2 e5 7.O-O Nc6.","From here White scored 53% in those games, with 13% drawn."]}}